      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "5\n4 0\n2 2\n5 4\n6 7\n1 0",
        "output": "1 2 3 4\n-1\n3 2 5 1 4\n2 5 4 1 6 3\n1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: For A=[1,2,3,4]A = [1, 2, 3, 4]A=[1,2,3,4], we don't need any swaps. So, we have f(A)=0=Kf(A) = 0 = Kf(A)=0=K, as needed.\nTest case 222: There are two possible arrays with N=2N = 2N=2: [1,2][1, 2][1,2] and [2,1][2, 1][2,1].\n\nFor A=[1,2]A = [1, 2]A=[1,2], we have f(A)=0f(A) = 0f(A)=0 since no swaps are needed.\nFor A=[2,1]A = [2, 1]A=[2,1], we're forced to swap 111 and 222 to sort it. Since 1⊕2=31\\oplus 2 = 31⊕2=3, we need to choose some KKK that's ≥3\\ge 3≥3. Of course, the smallest valid choice is hence K=3K = 3K=3; so we obtain f(A)=3f(A) = 3f(A)=3.\nIt can hence be seen that there's no length 222 array satisfying the conditions, so we print −1-1−1.\n\nTest case 333: For A=[3,2,5,1,4]A = [3, 2, 5, 1, 4]A=[3,2,5,1,4], it can be proved that AAA can be sorted in ascending order by choosing K=4K = 4K=4, whereas it cannot be sorted with K=3K = 3K=3.\nThis makes f(A)=4f(A) = 4f(A)=4, as required."
